1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,863
Every four years,
we add a whole extra day.

2
00:00:03,079 --> 00:00:03,629
Why?

3
00:00:03,939 --> 00:00:07,849
Because a year is not exactly 365 days.

4
00:00:08,205 --> 00:00:12,915
Earth takes about 365 and a
quarter days to circle the sun.

5
00:00:13,186 --> 00:00:16,096
That quarter day is easy to ignore,
but it adds up.

6
00:00:16,317 --> 00:00:19,947
After four years, the calendar
is a full day behind the seasons.

7
00:00:20,297 --> 00:00:25,667
In 45 BC, Julius Caesar fixed it
with one extra day every four years.

8
00:00:25,993 --> 00:00:28,083
But a quarter day is slightly too much.

9
00:00:28,349 --> 00:00:30,019
The real number is a bit smaller.

10
00:00:30,409 --> 00:00:34,999
The mistake was only eleven minutes a
year, but over centuries it piled up.

11
00:00:35,230 --> 00:00:39,020
By the 1500s,
the calendar had drifted about ten days.

12
00:00:39,371 --> 00:00:43,141
In 1582, Pope Gregory the
thirteenth made a new rule.

13
00:00:43,437 --> 00:00:45,967
Ten days were simply
deleted from the calendar.

14
00:00:46,327 --> 00:00:49,387
Years divisible by 100 are not leap years,

15
00:00:49,387 --> 00:00:52,557
unless they are also divisible by 400.

16
00:00:53,013 --> 00:00:57,343
So 1900 was not a leap year,
but the year 2000 was.

17
00:00:57,674 --> 00:01:01,104
Britain and India followed only in 1752.

18
00:01:01,424 --> 00:01:03,314
So a leap day is not a free gift.

19
00:01:03,650 --> 00:01:06,360
It is the calendar paying
back what our planet owes.

